From 493c6d151c2b256f0ee8f0b32e46dce0d90f0334 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Nicole Muzzin <nicole@muzz.in>
Date: Sun, 4 Oct 2026 05:57:27 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] layout: answer geometry queries without rebuilding the
 stacking context tree

offsetWidth, getBoundingClientRect(), getClientRects() and scroll offset
queries asked for stacking context tree construction, so the first such
query after any DOM change rebuilt the tree for the whole page. On a
20000-node page that was 15-25 ms per forced reflow even when the change
sat inside a contain:strict box and layout itself reused every cached
fragment.

These queries now build the tree only when the queried node needs it:
offset* when a sticky box applies to the node, getBoundingClientRect()
when a transform, perspective or sticky box applies, a fixed box sits in
a containing block other than the viewport, or a scroll container below
the viewport is scrolled. Otherwise the transform is the viewport scroll
offset that the rebuilt tree would carry over (clamped the same way), and
a scroll offset query only rebuilds the tree for a nonzero offset, the
only kind clamping can change.

The cumulative containing blocks these queries read are no longer left
to tree construction either. After layout they are marked stale, and a
query computes them along the path to the queried node: the walk only
descends into anonymous fragments and the fragments of the node's flat
tree ancestors, and falls back to walking every fragment if it doesn't
reach the node. The Layout::ensure_stacking_context_tree hook that
IntersectionObserver used goes away, since query_box_area now does this
itself.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
